const faq = [
  { q: 'What is the three-legged raven in Chinese mythology?', a: 'The three-legged raven (or crow) — called Yangwu (陽烏, "sun crow") or Jīnyā (金鴉, "golden crow") in Chinese — is a solar deity or spirit said to inhabit the suns. In classical Chinese cosmology, there were originally ten suns, each containing a three-legged bird. The archer Yi (后羿) shot down nine of the ten suns (and their birds) to save humanity from scorching — a myth recorded in the Huainanzi (c. 139 BCE). One sun (and one bird) was spared, becoming the present sun.' },
  { q: 'Is the Chinese sun crow a raven or a crow?', a: 'The distinction between raven and crow was not always made in classical Chinese texts — the character 烏 (wū) can mean both raven and crow. The solar bird is typically described as black and three-legged; some art traditions render it as a large, raven-like bird. Scholars generally translate Yangwu as "sun crow" or "sun raven" depending on context. The key symbolic point is a large black corvid as a solar emblem — a strikingly different symbolic role from the Western tradition where ravens are associated with darkness and death.' },
  { q: 'How is the Chinese three-legged raven related to the Japanese Yatagarasu?', a: 'The Japanese three-legged crow, Yatagarasu (八咫烏), is directly derived from the Chinese solar bird via cultural transmission across the Korean peninsula. In Japan, Yatagarasu serves as a divine guide (guiding Emperor Jimmu in Japanese foundation mythology) and is associated with the Kumano Sanzan shrines. The three-legged form is shared; the symbolic function shifts from solar embodiment (Chinese) to divine guidance (Japanese). Both traditions derive ultimately from a broader East Asian solar bird symbolism.' },
];
